notes.body:
Civil Engineering Design 1 (CIVE40002) is the first of the design modules that run through the first three years of Imperial's MEng Civil Engineering. It is a core 7.5-ECTS module across the autumn and spring terms. The programme specification says the Civil Engineering Design modules in the first three years are co-taught by leading consultants. It also says each of the first three years involves design projects that require regular presentations to "clients".

What the module aims to do
The module record (reviewed 2025–26) describes group, problem-based design work on real projects. Through it, students:
- build core engineering skills and see why the theory taught elsewhere matters in practice;
- develop conceptual designs as a group and practise creative design thinking;
- generate ideas and test whether design concepts are feasible;
- communicate ideas through hands-on modelling;
- plan and manage the group construction of a full-scale (1:1) design;
- apply geometric and parametric modelling;
- give and receive critical feedback on design responses to a brief;
- reflect on the civil engineer's role as both designer and maker.

The same record says the work is concentrated into two intensive Creative Design weeks, with no other teaching during those weeks, working in groups at several scales.

What this means for you
- There is no fixed lecture syllabus to revise and, as far as the published information shows, no written exam to cram for. You learn by doing, in a team, under time pressure.
- Assessment details (deliverables, weights, deadlines) are given in the module's own briefs. We have not stated them because they are not published on the pages we could see. Read each brief carefully and check what is being marked: often the process, the communication and the reflection, not just the final object.

How to prepare
- Practise freehand sketching before the design weeks (topic 2). Confidence with a pencil makes the first days far more productive.
- Revise the basic structural ideas from your mechanics and structures modules: equilibrium, load paths, bending, buckling (topic 3). Design weeks are where you apply them.
- Agree team roles and a daily plan at the start of each week, and keep a shared record of decisions and the reasons behind them. This makes the reflective writing much easier.
- Expect criticism of your work, and practise giving it constructively.
